Water refraction quality, receiver stability and HDR composition
==============================================================

Source requirements: user direction on 2026-10-03.
Parent: PBR.BaselineShading.todo / Implement water refraction integrated with PBR liquids and OIT.
Current implementation and evidence: PBR.WaterRefraction.md.
Related contracts: PBR.WaterMedium.md, PBR.Liquids.md,
  PBR.MaterialColorAndDisplay.md, Rendering.AuthoritativePipelineState.todo.
Document paths here are relative to this file's directory.

Goal and fixed requirements
  Retain geometric ray-marched refraction at the three higher water-quality levels. Provide exactly
  four levels, ordered highest to lowest: ray march x8, ray march x4, ray march x2, UV distortion.
  Every level uses scene-linear HDR water composition, refracted scattering direction, and optional
  downsampled background buffers. Preserve the independent WaterRefractionEnabled switch.
  Implement shared bilateral receiver filtering for all four quality levels at every supported
  background resolution, retaining valid receiver samples while excluding foreground contamination.
  Prevent avoidable receiver/confidence failures and loss of visible distortion. Do not treat
  invalid depth, foreground geometry or missing screen-space coverage as valid ray intersections.
  Quality algorithms and HDR migration remain unimplemented. Baseline diagnostics and design
  contracts are recorded in PBR.WaterRefraction.md; completion requires the verification gates below.

Scope and execution contract
  Reuse liquid, composite, water-medium, capture, framebuffer, shader-library and settings owners.
  Keep resource orchestration thin; separate receiver sampling, traversal, transport and composition.
  Preserve engine rendering order, first-person capture behavior, waves/flow, non-water liquids,
  atmosphere, shadows, world lifecycle and operation with LumOn both enabled and disabled.
  Follow repository documentation/comment conventions. Delegate all builds and tests to subagents.
  Do not launch Vintage Story; user supplies live visual acceptance. Keep source review, headless
  GPU checks, user-run results and measured performance distinct. Do not reuse old test counts as
  evidence for changed algorithms. Record implementation evidence in PBR.WaterRefraction.md.
  Scene reflections, caustics, exact animated camera waterline and native HDR monitor presentation
  remain separately owned tasks. Internal HDR water must work on ordinary SDR displays.
  Do not add temporal history, offscreen geometry tracing or a render graph without a separate
  design decision. Current opaque snapshots cannot supply arbitrary hidden transparent geometry.

Design decisions established by Phase 1
  - x8/x4/x2 are total maximum ray-position depth evaluations, including refinement and final
    revalidation; no hidden extra refinement loop. Filter taps and bounded UV fallback work are
    separate costs. Current tracing can perform 38 evaluations (32 + 5 + final lookup).
  - WaterRefractionQuality uses stable integer values 3=x8 (default), 2=x4, 1=x2, 0=UV distortion.
    WaterRefractionBackgroundScale independently selects divisor 1=full (default), 2=half dimensions
    rounded up. Missing/invalid values use defaults; preserve the existing enable flag, default false.
    Off is not a quality level. Exact migration/lifetime contract: PBR.WaterRefraction.md,
    Water quality and receiver contract. Runtime fields are implemented in Phase 6.
  - Continuity policy: valid ray result first, validated UV-distorted receiver when ray coverage is
    unavailable, safe undistorted transport only if neither has usable coverage. Fix avoidable
    failures first; the UV result remains an approximate receiver, never a confirmed ray hit.
  - Bilateral receiver filtering is required. Its weights, eligibility tests and tolerances must
    satisfy the local interface-plane geometry contract; these details remain implementation choices.

Bilateral receiver filtering: meaning and limitations
  A filtered background lookup typically combines four neighbouring texels. Ordinary bilinear
  filtering uses only their distance from the lookup position. A bilateral receiver filter also
  considers geometry: discard or reduce weights for texels that belong in front of the water or
  otherwise violate receiver eligibility, then normalize the surviving weights. Apply compatible
  weights to depth and scene-linear colour so foreground colour cannot leak into a water receiver.
  Example: three taps see the submerged floor and one sees a foreground rock. Retaining the three
  valid taps can yield a smooth floor sample without rejecting the whole lookup or importing rock
  colour. No surviving weights means unavailable coverage, not permission to sample arbitrary colour.
  Filtering must not average unrelated depth layers into a fictional ray intersection. Previously
  downsampled colour contamination cannot be undone merely by rejecting taps at lookup time.

Phase 1: Establish reproducible cases and shared contracts
  Outcome: bounded quality, sampling and HDR contracts that subsequent work can implement.
  Completed 2026-10-03: diagnostic baseline, settled settings/sampling contracts and HDR inventory
    in PBR.WaterRefraction.md. Fresh build and 114/114 focused regressions passed without skips.
    Second review and independent audit-stage-completion found no remaining Phase 1 issues.
    No live appearance/performance acceptance or implementation of later phases is claimed.

