Phase 2: Establish shared HDR water transport and composition
  Dependencies: Phase 1 HDR producer/consumer contract and its identified shared prerequisites.
  Entry gate: PBR.WaterRefraction.md / HDR producer and consumer contract. The common HDR handoff
    remains unimplemented; complete it in the parent scene-HDR task before starting this phase.
  Outcome: one scene-linear composition path usable by all four sampling algorithms.

  ☐ Preserve HDR radiance through water surface lighting, RGB medium transport, fallback blending
    and transparency composition. Remove liquid-local display resolve/dither at this boundary.
    ☐ Retain values above one and apply exposure/output conversion once at the agreed final boundary.
    ☐ Preserve RGB background transmission and avoid adding the original background twice.
    ☐ Define interaction with ordinary transparent objects, overlapping water and late first-person
      draws; document remaining ordered-transport limitations rather than claiming OIT solves them.
    ☐ Preserve straight-through disabled-refraction rendering within the compatible colour contract.
  ☐ Separate background receiver selection from shared optical evaluation and HDR composition.
    ☐ Carry receiver validity, sampling method, submerged length and refracted direction explicitly;
      do not let an approximate UV receiver masquerade as an exact ray intersection.
    ☐ Use the refracted direction within the water medium for directional single scattering.
      Establish photon-direction signs and world/view transforms; distinguish above-water entry
      from underwater exit, where the outgoing refracted segment is air, not the scattering medium.
    ☐ Preserve water IOR/Fresnel/TIR, RGB extinction, local/environment sources and air-segment
      atmospheric transport without double-applying opaque water-volume attenuation.
  ☐ Validate HDR highlight preservation, linear blends, exposure independence, single display
    conversion, colored transmission and independently predicted directional-scattering cases.
  ☐ Complete focused regression checks and source review before sampler implementations depend on it.

Phase 3: Implement coherent background resolution and receiver filtering
  Dependencies: shared HDR and receiver contracts.
  Outcome: coherent full/downsampled receiver data shared by all four levels.

  ☐ Extend existing snapshot owners with the selected optional background downsampling control.
    ☐ Keep colour/depth/validity in one frame, projection and resolution generation, including
      restored pre-overlay pixels. Resolve capture/downsample ordering before publication.
    ☐ Define a geometry-aware reduction policy preserving colour/depth association and excluding
      sky, foreground and overlay metadata contamination; do not blindly average depth layers.
    ☐ Distinguish framebuffer dimensions from background dimensions in projection, texel offsets,
      filtering footprints and UV bounds. Cover odd dimensions and resize transitions.
    ☐ Reuse framebuffer/binding ownership APIs; never sample an active writable attachment.
  ☐ Implement shared bilateral receiver filtering for all four quality levels and supported
    background resolutions, replacing the existing nearest/all-neighbours rejection policy.
    ☐ Compare interface-plane eligibility and slope/depth tolerances using executed shore/occluder
      cases. Select and document a bounded policy that retains valid taps without invented surfaces.
    ☐ Share eligible weights between colour and depth; preserve hard rejection of unsupported data.
    ☐ Verify mixed foreground/background taps retain valid receiver support without colour leakage,
      all-invalid taps report unavailable coverage, and unrelated depth layers do not create false hits.
  ☐ Verify repeated resolution/quality/toggle changes, resize/reload, failed publication, world
    teardown and LumOn switching without stale sampling, leaks or redundant steady-state allocation.
  ☐ Validate full versus reduced resolution at thin silhouettes, slopes and first-person boundaries;
    record memory, reduction-pass and lookup costs separately. Review before proceeding.

Phase 4: Implement lowest-quality UV distortion
  Dependencies: shared HDR transport and coherent receiver sampling.
  Outcome: lowest quality uses bounded UV distortion with no iterative ray traversal.

  ☐ Implement material-normal-based screen UV distortion with projection/FOV/aspect correction,
    water-thickness/depth-bias suppression and safe sampling bounds using the existing water optics.
    ☐ Select and document IOR-based versus pixel-normal-offset behavior against flat/wavy-water
      references. Express all dimensional parameters consistently in VGE's metre units.
    ☐ Validate distorted receivers with the shared filter; define safe undistorted fallback when
      all candidate coverage is unavailable and prevent foreground smearing at clamped edges.
    ☐ Supply a documented approximate water path length for transport, with refracted scattering
      direction even though UV selection is not a geometric ray intersection.
    ☐ Preserve underwater entry/exit and TIR; distortion must not imply transmission during TIR.
  ☐ Verify predictable UV displacement, depth suppression, aspect/FOV changes, entry/exit, HDR
    composition and both background resolutions. Review the implementation and bounded lookup cost.

Phase 5: Implement bounded x8/x4/x2 traversal and prevent avoidable distortion loss
  Dependencies: shared receiver sampling and validated UV fallback.
  Outcome: higher quality keeps geometric tracing within exact budgets and stable receiver selection.

  ☐ Replace the 32-step plus five-refinement algorithm with the agreed total 8/4/2 budgets.
    ☐ Choose sample placement, ray extent and crossing estimation based on represented depth and
      projected coverage; do not merely delete samples from the old fixed 32-metre quadratic march.
    ☐ Use analytic estimates or budgeted refinement where appropriate; bound and count every
      additional ray-depth evaluation. Report filtering/fallback taps separately from ray steps.
    ☐ Preserve oriented-interface eligibility, positive view-Z rays and camera-transform invariance.
  ☐ Fix demonstrated false-negative receiver tests and confidence-driven loss of valid distortion.
    ☐ Replace blanket adjacent-tap rejection where valid receiver support remains available.
    ☐ Establish depth/residual tolerances from sampling footprint and geometry; do not just enlarge
      the old 0.15-metre threshold until foreground/discontinuity crossings are accepted.
    ☐ Remove unconditional edge/range confidence attenuation for otherwise supported receivers;
      separate true geometric/data validity from aesthetic blending confidence.
    ☐ Avoid aborting a recoverable search at an invalid intermediate sample when remaining budget
      and known coverage support a safe search; never bridge unknown topology as a confirmed hit.
    ☐ Integrate the agreed validated UV fallback for genuinely unavailable ray coverage. Transition
      between complete HDR transport results without darkening, double backgrounds or edge jumps.
    ☐ Classify irreducible cases explicitly: offscreen/occluded receivers, sky/invalid snapshots,
      no valid UV support and TIR. Zero transmission during TIR is correct, not confidence failure.
  ☐ Verify exact iteration ceilings and absence of the old hidden refinement loop for all tiers.
  ☐ Re-run baseline reproductions and compare against an offline higher-budget reference for
    receiver location, missed thin geometry, motion continuity and fallback frequency.
    ☐ Demonstrate retained distortion for the reproduced avoidable-failure cases; do not claim
      universal receiver availability from screen-space-only data.
  ☐ Complete focused tests and source review for each tier before claiming traversal completion.

